WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.020
When the skin of a mouse's hindpaw is indented, what climbs the spinal cord?

2
00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:11.600
The textbook draws a cable.

3
00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:15.760
Touch spikes enter the dorsal column and run up to the brainstem.

4
00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:19.280
Studies in mice show a missing branch.

5
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:23.400
Some of the spikes have already been through the cord's own circuitry.

6
00:00:24.383 --> 00:00:26.743
One route really is direct.

7
00:00:27.903 --> 00:00:30.763
Touch afferents are the sensory fibres from the skin.

8
00:00:31.583 --> 00:00:36.803
Some run up the dorsal column with no synapse on the way, and reach the gracile

9
00:00:36.803 --> 00:00:38.203
nucleus in the brainstem.

10
00:00:39.243 --> 00:00:45.863
In anaesthetized adult mice, this direct input carried vibration with high temporal precision.

12
00:00:46.267 --> 00:00:51.647
Touch afferents also end in the dorsal horn, a layered region of the cord.

13
00:00:52.347 --> 00:00:55.387
The layers are called laminae, numbered from the surface.

14
00:00:56.367 --> 00:01:02.387
In the mouse lumbar cord, about 70% of the neurons in this zone are excitatory, and

15
00:01:02.387 --> 00:01:04.487
about 30% are inhibitory.

16
00:01:05.447 --> 00:01:12.287
A postsynaptic dorsal column neuron, or PSDC neuron, gets its excitation mainly from

17
00:01:12.287 --> 00:01:13.407
these local neurons.

18
00:01:13.407 --> 00:01:16.447
It sends an axon up the dorsal column.

19
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:22.460
Two kinds of inhibition shape what the PSDC neuron sends.

20
00:01:23.380 --> 00:01:29.080
In feedforward inhibition, the same afferent input that excites the neuron also

21
00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:31.560
excites an interneuron that inhibits it.

22
00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:37.600
In presynaptic inhibition, the inhibition lands on the afferent terminal and turns

23
00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:39.320
down the transmitter it releases.

24
00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:46.140
In anaesthetized adult mice, a genetic knockout of either one enlarged the receptive

25
00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:47.240
fields of these neurons.

26
00:01:48.150 --> 00:01:52.150
Now compare arrival times, using illustrative numbers.

27
00:01:53.030 --> 00:01:58.230
The direct route is 0.08 m at 16 m/s.

28
00:01:58.630 --> 00:02:00.150
That takes 5 ms.

29
00:02:01.070 --> 00:02:05.230
The indirect route needs 2.5 ms to reach the cord.

30
00:02:05.970 --> 00:02:09.910
Two synapses, at an assumed 1 ms each, add 2.

31
00:02:10.490 --> 00:02:15.350
The PSDC axon, at an assumed 8 m/s, adds 5.

32
00:02:15.910 --> 00:02:18.610
The total is 9.5 ms.

33
00:02:19.250 --> 00:02:22.990
So the processed copy arrives 4.5 ms later.

34
00:02:23.950 --> 00:02:26.230
The two copies meet again.

35
00:02:27.050 --> 00:02:38.370
In anaesthetized adult mice, the direct and indirect inputs to single thalamus-projecting neurons in the gracile nucleus had highly aligned receptive fields.

37
00:02:39.030 --> 00:02:41.170
The direct input carried vibration.

38
00:02:41.910 --> 00:02:46.610
The indirect input carried sustained indentation, up to high forces.

39
00:02:47.530 --> 00:02:49.730
Both contributed to gentle displacement.

40
00:02:49.730 --> 00:02:54.970
So the brainstem receives a direct copy and a processed copy.
